{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/norm-and-normed-vector-space.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/norm-and-normed-vector-space.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of function is a norm on a vector space $\\boldsymbol{V}$?","options":["A function $\\boldsymbol{V}\\to\\boldsymbol{V}$ that reverses vectors.","A function $\\|\\cdot\\|\\colon \\boldsymbol{V}\\to[0,+\\infty)$ satisfying the norm axioms.","A scalar product that must be linear in both arguments.","A metric that is defined on arbitrary pairs without reference to vectors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9e81930ce2a211a4f2a32dd5"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which axiom says that $\\|c\\boldsymbol{x}\\| = |c|\\,\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$?","options":["Positive homogeneity","Definiteness","Triangle inequality","Pointwise convergence"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"494531fbb9d74d27747f37ef"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In $\\mathbb{R}$ with the absolute-value norm, what is $\\|-3\\|$?","options":["$-3$","$3$","$0$","$9$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fb6ae3e512c24f85405343c0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which of the following is a normed vector space?","options":["An arbitrary set with no vector addition","A sequence of functions with no norm","$\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the Euclidean norm","A boundary with no ambient space"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"206e46f922708abf41e3f83b"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does definiteness of a norm require?","options":["$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\| = 0$ for every vector $\\boldsymbol{x}$.","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$ may be negative for some vectors.","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\| = 0$ if and only if $\\boldsymbol{x} = \\boldsymbol{0}$.","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\| = \\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|+\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$ for all vectors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a937e8ac16fdf0035ad4989c"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does a normed vector space naturally become a metric space?","options":["By defining $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$.","By defining $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|\\,\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$.","By ignoring the vector-space structure.","By defining $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\|\\boldsymbol{x}-\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8567b1693f07bea90404d2e1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\| = 5$, what is $\\|-2\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$ in any normed vector space?","options":["$-10$","$2$","$10$","$7$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"99215485cfb2cc1525d8175c"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\| = 3$ and $\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\| = 4$, what upper bound follows from the triangle inequality?","options":["$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|\\leq 7$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\| = 12$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|\\geq 7$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|\\leq 1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d7f065d0e8482f024b50647b"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A proposed length function satisfies non-negativity and positive homogeneity, but has $N(\\boldsymbol{x}) = 0$ for some nonzero $\\boldsymbol{x}$. Why is it not a norm?","options":["It fails because norms must take negative values.","It fails definiteness.","It fails because every norm must be Euclidean.","It fails because no vector space can have a zero vector."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8974e90bfdf35f80ec2ec1cc"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement is the best interpretation of a normed vector space?","options":["It is a vector space equipped with a rule assigning consistent lengths to vectors.","It is any metric space, even without vector addition or scalar multiplication.","It is a vector space in which all vectors have norm $1$.","It is a vector space where the triangle inequality is replaced by equality."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef0a76fa78fc799c67d8d3e6"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $N(x) = x^2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$. Which norm axiom fails?","options":["Non-negativity","The existence of scalar multiplication","The existence of the zero vector","Positive homogeneity"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2e5678bf93055bb970636d23"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_40ba0313_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A function $d(x,y)=\\|x+y\\|$ is proposed as the metric induced by a norm. Which metric axiom fails immediately?","options":["Symmetry, since $\\|x+y\\|$ is never equal to $\\|y+x\\|$.","Non-negativity, since norms can be negative.","The triangle inequality, because sums are not allowed in metrics.","Identity of indiscernibles, since $d(x,x)=\\|2x\\|$ need not be $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"267e8b14e7a14137a9ea3113"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a norm assign to each vector?","options":["A boundary point.","A nonnegative length.","An open cover.","A partial derivative."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4bb410d5b9a0c500454937ed"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which property is positive homogeneity of a norm?","options":["$\\|c\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=|c|\\,\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|=\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=0$ for every vector","$\\|c\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=c+\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ee988f401b04867bfe7e20e0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does it take for $(\\boldsymbol V,\\|\\cdot\\|)$ to be a normed vector space?","options":["$\\boldsymbol V$ is a set with no operations.","$\\boldsymbol V$ is a vector space equipped with a norm.","$\\boldsymbol V$ is an open cover.","$\\boldsymbol V$ contains only one vector."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f62e465172c4761c0bbf981f"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=4$, what is $\\|3\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$?","options":["$7$","$4$","$12$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"36a7b5b8517dcbfa3c9f814d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=0$, what follows from definiteness?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is any vector.","$\\boldsymbol{x}=\\boldsymbol{0}$","$\\boldsymbol{x}$ has norm $1$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is outside the vector space."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"29981f7119761d3586505797"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Given a normed vector space, which formula defines a metric from the norm?","options":["$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})=\\|\\boldsymbol{x}-\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})=\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})=\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})=0$ for all vectors"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2073a81b72ef409f610a51cc"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which norm axiom gives the triangle inequality?","options":["$\\|c\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=|c|\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=0$ iff $\\boldsymbol{x}=\\boldsymbol{0}$","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|\\ge0$ only","$\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|\\le\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|+\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"378b055e8a7d03fa666cdefe"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_norm_and_normed_vector_space_147a7d0f_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=2$ and $\\|\\boldsymbol{y}\\|=5$, what upper bound follows for $\\|\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}\\|$?","options":["$10$","$7$","$3$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"369cb57348a193b6d5946391"}]}}